              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong quantale
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suplattice"} suplattice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+symmetric+monoidal+category"} closed symmetric monoidal category
            of suplattices where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maps"} maps
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+colimit"} preserve
            arbitrary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joins"} joins
          %p
            In most recent works, quantales are defined more generally as
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semigroup"} semi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+objects"} group objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} in
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            of suplattices.
          %p
            In more detail, a quantale is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            having all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joins"} joins
            and an associative,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+category"} distributes
            over joins (the internal-homs then come automatically by the
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
          %p
            The internal-homs in a quantale are sometimes called
            %em residuations
            and written
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo \
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\backslash y
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y/x
          %p
            As a semigroup (monoid if unital) in suplattices, a quantale is essentially the same thing as an 1-object
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantaloid"} quantaloid
          %h2#quantales_and_frames Quantales and Frames
          %p Additional conditions often imposed on a quantale include:
          %ul
            %li
              Commutativity:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\otimes y = y\otimes x
            %li
              Idempotence:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\otimes x = x
            %li
              Affineness: the unit for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
              is the top element:
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mo ⊤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1=\top
          %p
            (On affineness: see also
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicartesian+monoidal+category"} semicartesian monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            is forced to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meet"} meet
            (whence commutativity is automatic) and the quantale is thereby a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#meet"}
            below. General quantales are sometimes considered to be a “noncommutative” version of a frame, whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            would be a category of “noncommutative
            = succeed "s.\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
          %p
            (This is the origin of the name “quantale,” a
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portmanteau"} portmanteau
            of “quantum” and “locale”. Note, though, that quantales seem to be generally treated in the literature more as “quantum frames” than “quantum locales,” and in particular their morphisms usually go in the “frame direction.” Possibly this can be explained by the fact that in the past, it was common to use the word “locale” for what we now call a “frame” and simply distinguish between “locale homomorphisms” (now called “frame homomorphisms”) and “continuous maps.” The name “quantale” was introduced by C.J. Mulvey.)
          #meet.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
              is idempotent and affine, then the monoid multiplication is the meet operation.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M, \otimes, 1)
              be the monoid. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq x
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq y
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = a \otimes a \leq x \otimes y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \otimes y \leq x \otimes 1 = x
              and similarly
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi y
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \otimes y \leq y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq x \otimes y
              implies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq y
              by transitivity. Thus
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq x \otimes y
              iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq x
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \otimes y
              satisfies the defining property of the meet
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge y
          %p Along similar lines, the following construction provides passage from commutative affine quantales to frames:
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Q, \cdot, 1)
              be a commutative affine quantale, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Idem
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem(Q)
              be the subposet of elements
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \cdot x = x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Idem
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem(Q)
              is a frame, where the meet operation is given by multiplication in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Idem
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem
              is right adjoint to the forgetful functor from frames to commutative affine quantales.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y
              are idempotent, then so is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \cdot y
              using the fact that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
              is commutative. Thus
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Idem
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem(Q)
              is an idempotent affine submonoid of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#meet"}
              forces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
              to be the meet. Next, we show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Idem
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem(Q)
              is closed under taking joins in
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              is a collection of idempotents, we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo ≤
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                          %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                            %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    x_i \leq x_i x_i
                    \\leq
                    \\Big(
                    \\textstyle{\bigvee_i}
                    x_i
                    \\Big)
                    \\Big(
                    \\textstyle{\bigvee_i}
                    x_i
                    \\Big)
            %p
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                          %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≤
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                            %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                              %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\textstyle{\bigvee_i} x_i
                    \\;\leq\;
                    (
                    \\textstyle{\bigvee_i} x_i
                    )
                    (
                    \\textstyle{\bigvee_i} x_i
                    ),
            %p
              which is all we need (the opposite inequality is automatic since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \cdot a \leq a \cdot 1 = a
              for all
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in Q
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Idem
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem(Q)
              are calculated just as they are in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              distributes over arbitrary joins, we have that binary meets distribute over arbitrary joins in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Idem
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem(Q)
            %p
              Finally, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a frame and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              is a commutative affine quantale, it is clear that a quantale map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon A \to Q
              takes elements in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              (which are idempotent under meet) to idempotents in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              factors uniquely through
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Idem
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem(Q) \hookrightarrow Q
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi Idem
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to Idem(Q)
              is a frame map. This shows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Idem
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem
              is the right adjoint as claimed.
          %p
            In fact, we may also observe that the forgetful functor from commutative affine quantales to commutative quantales also has a right adjoint, just by passing from a commutative quantale to the principal downset given by the quantale unit. (However, the forgetful functor from commutative quantales to quantales does
            %em not
            have a right adjoint.)
          %p
            On very general grounds, the forgetful functor from the category of frames to the category of quantales has a left adjoint (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits+in+categories+of+algebras#relatively_free_functors"} here
          %h2#quantale_congruences_and_nuclei Quantale congruences and nuclei
          %p
            A
            %em quantale congruence
            is simply an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ≡
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ({\equiv}) \hookrightarrow Q \times Q
            on a quantale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            that respects the quantale multiplication and the taking of sups: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \equiv b
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \equiv y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi b
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a x \equiv b y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ≡
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i \equiv y_i
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in I
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo ≡
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigvee_{i \in I} x_i \equiv \bigvee_{i \in I} y_i
          %p
            Consequently,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo ≡
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q/{\equiv}
            defines a quantale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{Q}
            with operations inherited along a quantale quotient map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo :
                    %mi Q
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: Q \to \tilde{Q}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            preserves arbitrary sups, it has a right adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo :
                  %mover
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r: \tilde{Q} \to Q
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
            applied to posets. Thus we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closure+operator"} closure operator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mi q
                    %mo :
                    %mi Q
                    %mo →
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r q: Q \to Q
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{Q}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo :
                  %mover
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r: \tilde{Q} \to Q
            is isomorphic to the inclusion
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Fix
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi r
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fix(r q) \hookrightarrow Q
          %p
            The monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r q
            is a quantale nucleus in the sense of the following definition:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %mi Q
                    %mo →
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j: Q \to Q
              on a quantale
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              is a
              %em nucleus
              if it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
              (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
              implies
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j(x) \leq j(y)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq j(x)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j j(x) = j(x)
              for all
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in Q
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq j(1)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j(x) \cdot j(y) \leq j(x \cdot y)
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in Q
          %p
            There is a natural bijective correspondence between congruences on a quantale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            and nuclei on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
          %h2#enrichment_over_quantales Enrichment over quantales
          %p
            Aside from being “noncommutative frames”, a different way of thinking about quantales views them as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-category"} (0,1)-categorical
            analogue of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmos"} cosmos
            (in the sense of Benabou). In particular, one can then study
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            over a quantale. A classic example is Lawvere
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ≥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ([0, \infty], \geq)
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            taken as tensor product.
          %p
            For a quantale
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            = succeed "-Cat" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            = succeed "-functors." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,d_X)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a set and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_x\colon X \times X \to Q
            is a symmetric and transitive
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon (X,d_X) \to (Y,d_Y)
            are non-expansive functions (namely
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_X(x_1,x_2) \leq d_Y(f(y_1,y_2))
          %p
            When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is a commutative affine quantale,
            = succeed "-Cat" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,d_X)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y,d_Y)
            are given respectively by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X\times Y, d^\wedge_{X\times Y})
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X\times Y, d^\otimes_{X\times Y})
            where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d^\wedge_{X\times Y}(x_1,y_1,x_2,y_2):= d_X(x_1,x_2) \wedge d_Y(y_1,y_2)
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi d
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mtext .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d^\otimes_{X\times Y}(x_1,y_1,x_2,y_2):= d_X(x_1,x_2) \otimes d_Y(y_1,y_2)\text{.}
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is idempotent (i.e.,
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo =
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge=\otimes
            = succeed "-Cat" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
          %p
            The converse implication does not hold in general, as discussed in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Clementino2003"} Clementino &amp; Hofmann &amp; Tholen (2003)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Clementino2006"} Clementino &amp; Hofmann (2006)
            %a{:href => "#Clementino2009"} Clementino &amp; Hofmann &amp; Stubbe (2009)
            and
            %a{:href => "#Stubbe25"} Stubbe &amp; Yu
            in the more general context of
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantaloid"} quantaloid
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Stubbe25"} Stubbe &amp; Yu
            = succeed "-Cat" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              be a quantale, then
              = succeed "-Cat" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the underlying sup-lattice of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                  is a frame (i.e.,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∧
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a\wedge -)
                  has a right adjoint for every
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in Q
                  or equivalently
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∧
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
                  distributes over arbitrary joins);
              %li
                %p
                  for every
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b,c\in Q
                  then
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi c
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo ∧
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∨
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \otimes b)\wedge c= ((a \wedge c)\otimes (b\wedge 1) ) \vee ((1\wedge a)\otimes (b\wedge c))
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The above correspondence does not imply that if
              = succeed "-Cat" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              is idempotent. Indeed, the three element quantale
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo ⊤
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\top,v,\bot\}
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi v
                    %mo =
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v\otimes v= \bot
              satisfies the above conditions but it is not idempotent.
          %p
            Enrichment is often particularly interesting for
            = succeed "-quantales" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p#compsci
            Quantales are a surprisingly commonplace structure in computer science. See the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#automata"} automata references
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+monoid"} free monoid
            over some set of characters
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            to be the empty string, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \cdot b
            to be the join of two strings, the quantalic operations are then:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 = \{\epsilon\}
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi J
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi i
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \otimes J = \{ i\cdot j \mid i \in I, j \in J \}
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi J
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi J
                    %mi i
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K/J = \{ i \mid \forall_{j\in J} i\cdot j \in K \}
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo \
                    %mi K
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %mi i
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\backslash K = \{ j \mid \forall_{i\in I} i\cdot j \in K \}
          %p
            This example generalizes as follows: given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            (for instance, a monoid equipped with the discrete order, as in the previous example), the collection of down-closed subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            carries a quantale structure given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
            with respect to categories enriched in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi TV
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2} = TV
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            of truth values. Explicitly, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            denotes the unit of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
            the multiplication, then
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 = \{x\in M \mid x \leq e\}
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi J
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi J
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi i
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \otimes J = \{x\in M \mid \exists_{i \in I} \exists_{j \in J} x \leq i \cdot j\}
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi J
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi J
                    %mi i
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K/J = \{i \in M \mid \forall_{j\in J} i\cdot j \in K \}
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo \
                    %mi K
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %mi i
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\backslash K = \{j \in M \mid \forall_{i\in I} i\cdot j \in K \}
          %p
            Another class of examples: internal homs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mi sLat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom_{sLat}(X, X)
            in the closed monoidal category of suplattices. For example, when the suplattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a power set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(S)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mi sLat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom_{sLat}(P(S), P(S))
            with the poset of binary relations
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ×
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(S \times S)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An example in algebra is given by the lattice of ideals of a commutative ring, with the tensor product given by ideal multiplication, which makes it into a commutative affine quantale. Residuation in this case is
                %em ideal division
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝔞
                        %mo :
                        %mi 𝔟
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi x
                        %mi 𝔟
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi 𝔞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathfrak{a} : \mathfrak{b}) = \{x | x\mathfrak{b} \subseteq \mathfrak{a}\}
            %li
              %p
                The lattice of positive natural numbers
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi n
                        %mo ⇔
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \leq n \iff m\mid n
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                        %mi I
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %mrow
                          %mtable{:columnalign => "left left", :displaystyle => "false"}
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mtext lcm
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msub
                                  %mi m
                                  %mn 1
                                %mo ,
                                %mi …
                                %mo ,
                                %msub
                                  %mi m
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                                    %mi I
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo ,
                              %mtd
                                %mtext if
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mi I
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mtext is finite
                                %mo .
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mn 0
                                %mo ,
                              %mtd
                                %mi otherwise
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\bigvee_{I} m_i =
                      \\begin{cases}
                      \\text{lcm}(m_1,\dots,m_{|I|}),&amp;\text{if }\,I\,\text{ is finite}.\\
                      0,&amp;otherwise
                      \\end{cases}
              %p
                and meets given by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi gcd
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gcd
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mo ×
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}\times \mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mtext lcm
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi m
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi k
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mtext lcm
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi m
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{lcm}(k\cdot m,k\cdot n) = k\cdot \text{lcm}(m,n)
            %li
              %p
                The posets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,\infty]
                with the usual order structure
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
                of the real numbers are quantales under the usual multiplication of real numbers. This basically follows from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi sup
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi c
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msub
                        %mi sup
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sup_{s\in S}(c\cdot s) = c\cdot \sup_{s\in S}(s)
                for any
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\geq 0
                and subset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\subseteq \mathbb{R}
                (including unbounded sets).
          %p
            Quantales, as monoids in the symmetric monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sLat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sLat
          %h2#quantales
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            \-quantales
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-quantale" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            equipped with an additional structure of an involution
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi Q
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} * : Q \to Q
          %p
            for which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi y
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \otimes y)^* = y^* \otimes x^*
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 1
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1^* = 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            denotes the monoidal unit. (The operator is assumed to be covariant with respect to the poset structure.)
          %p
            An example of a
            = succeed "-quantale" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "-operation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^* = \{(y, x): (x, y) \in R\}
          %p
            Another example is obtained by taking the quantale of down-closed subsets of a
            = succeed "-monoidal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            (which is the same thing as a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-monoid"} monoid
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            of posets), with the quantale structure given by Day convolution as described above, and the
            = succeed "-operator" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            = succeed "-operator" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^* = \{x^*: x \in L\}
          %p
            A
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
              \-enriched category
            over a
            = succeed "-quantale" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, d: X \times X \to Q)
            enriched in the underlying quantale, such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(y, x) = d(x, y)^*
          %p
            This notion can also be expressed in terms of lax morphisms of
            = succeed "-quantales;" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
          %h2#RelationToLinearLogic Relation to linear logic
          %p
            A commutative quantale is in particular a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            (a symmetric monoidal
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-category"} (0,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            in the general sense. Precisely if it has a
            %a{:href => "dualizing+object+in+a+closed+category"} dualizing object
            then it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} star-autonomous category
            and hence a model for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            in the original sense. (see e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Yetter90"} Yetter 90, page 43
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
          %h2#morphisms_of_quantales Morphisms of quantales
          %p There is a variety of notions of morphism of quantale, just as there is a variety of notions of morphism between closed monoidal categories. All the notions considered here are morphisms between the underlying sup-lattices, in other words preserve arbitrary joins, hence are left adjoints as functors between the underlying categories.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                At the weak end of the scale, one may consider
                %em lax morphisms
                of quantales, i.e., (lax)
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal functor
              %ul
                %li
                  An important example of this is that
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} categories enriched
                  in a monoidal poset
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi M
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                  = succeed "s," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mi d
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mi M
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^d: 2^{M} \to 2^{X \times X}
                  where the domain is the quantale of upward-closed subsets of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                  with the Day convolution structure, and the codomain is the quantale of binary relations on
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                A stronger notion is of
                %em strong morphisms
                of quantales seen as monoidal categories. As noted above, all quantale morphisms considered here are already left adjoints in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi MonCat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MonCat
                (the 2-category of monoidal categories, lax monoidal functors, and monoidal transformations), then the left adjoint is strong monoidal. This often occurs in practice.
            %li
              %p An even stronger notion is where the morphisms also strongly preserve the closed structure, i.e., the internal homs or residuations.
            %li
              %p
                There are corresponding notions of morphisms of
                = succeed "-quantales," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                operations. For instance, the notion of
                = succeed "-enriched" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                = succeed "-monoidal" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                can be equivalently recast as a lax morphism between
                = succeed "-quantales," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi d
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mi M
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo ×
                          %mi X
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^d: 2^M \to 2^{X \times X}.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"}> locale
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantaloid"}> quantaloid
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiquantale"} semiquantale
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+over+a+quantale"} module over a quantale
          %h2#references References
          %p The initial paper to use the term `quantale' was
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christopher+J.+Mulvey"}> Christopher J. Mulvey
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "http://www.maths.sussex.ac.uk/Staff/CJM/research/pdf/&.pdf"} “&amp;”
                %a{:href => "http://zbmath.org/?q=an:0633.46065"} Zbl 0633.46065
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eom"}> eom
                \:
                %a{:href => "http://www.encyclopediaofmath.org/index.php/Quantale"} quantale
          %p
            Discussion of quantales as a model for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
          %ul
            %li#Yetter90
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Yetter"}> David Yetter
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantales and (noncommutative) linear logic
              %strong 55
              (1990) 41-64 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/2274953"}> doi:10.2307/2274953
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p A monograph on quantales:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kimmo+I.+Rosenthal"}> Kimmo I. Rosenthal
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantales and their applications
          %p#automata Relations with automata and process semantics
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samson+Abramsky"}> Samson Abramsky
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Vickers"} Steve Vickers
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantales, observational logic and process semantics
              %strong 3
              (1993) 161-227.
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129500000189"} doi
              %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.210.1485"}
                CiteSeer
                %sup X
          %p Connections to operator algebras and etale groupoids is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pedro+Resende"}> Pedro Resende
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Étale groupoids and their quantales
              %strong 208
              (2007) 147-209; also published electronically:
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2006.02.004"} doi
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0412478"} math/0412478
            %li
              M.C. Protin, P. Resende,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantales of open groupoids
              %strong 6
              (2012) 199–247.
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pedro+Resende"}> Pedro Resende
              , Lectures on étale groupoids, inverse semigroups and quantales, Lecture Notes for the GAMAP IP Meeting, Antwerp, 4–18 September, 2006, 115 pp.;
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.ist.utl.pt/%7Epmr/poci55958/gncg51gamap-version2.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pedro+Resende"}> Pedro Resende
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Groupoid sheaves as quantale sheaves
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0807.4848v3"} arxiv/0807.4848
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jpaa.2011.05.002"} doi
            %li
              D. Kruml, J.W. Pelletier, P. Resende,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+Rosick%C3%BD"} J. Rosický
              = succeed "," do
